# There is no modem here and no site to call, so what these show
# is their own face: the prompt, the settings, the log.

shot    aterm
cap     ATerm 2.6, the terminal emulator, reading its configuration from
cap     SYS/ATERM.
run     aterm
wait    6

shot    elm
for     elm
cap     Elm, the mail reader, opening on an empty mailbox -- which is the
cap     honest state of a mail system with no network.  Fourteen programs
cap     travel with it in CMDS/ELM, and they are on the three cards that
cap     follow.  Until 2026-08-29 those thirteen were spread across three
cap     cards that between them typed five of the names.
run     elm
wait    10
quit    q\r

shot    elm-suite
size    36 80
for     newalias printmail readmsg messages
cap     Four of the elm helpers, each asked for itself: `newalias' rebuilds
cap     the alias database, `printmail' prints a folder, `readmsg' picks
cap     one message out of one, `messages' counts what is waiting.  With an
cap     empty mailbox what they have to say is short and it is theirs.
run     newalias
wait    8
run     printmail
wait    8
run     readmsg
wait    8
run     messages
wait    8

shot    elm-away
size    36 80
for     answer arepdaemon autoreply fastmail filter
cap     The elm programs that act while you are not there: `autoreply' and
cap     `answer' reply for you, `arepdaemon' is the daemon that runs them,
cap     `filter' sorts arriving mail into folders, and `fastmail' is the
cap     bulk sender.  All five were credited to the elm-suite card and none
cap     was ever typed on it.
run     autoreply -?
wait    8
run     answer -?
wait    8
run     arepdaemon -?
wait    8
run     filter -?
wait    8
run     fastmail -?
wait    8

shot    finger
size    36 80
for     finger net osknet
cap     Half the network set, each asked for itself.  There is no interface
cap     configured here, so what they show is what they would do if there
cap     were -- and all three want /dd/startup.net, which is a file you
cap     write for your own network and no collection can ship.  The other
cap     three are on the `network' card; until 2026-08-29 these two cards
cap     credited the same six programs between them and ran four.
cap     `osknet' is last: it takes the session down behind it, so anything
cap     asked for after it would photograph nothing.
run     finger
wait    8
run     net
wait    8
run     osknet
wait    12
kill

shot    frm
size    36 80
for     frm checkalias listalias newmail
cap     `frm' lists the From lines of a folder and the alias tools read the
cap     alias files; `newmail' watches for arriving mail.  With no network
cap     and an empty mailbox, what each says about that is the card.
run     frm
wait    6
run     listalias
wait    6
run     checkalias root
wait    6
run     newmail -?
wait    6

shot    kermit
cap     Kermit, the file-transfer program.  With no serial line to talk to
cap     it says what it can do: connect, send, receive, or be the server.
run     kermit
wait    5

shot    kermit-family
for     kermit2 kermit3 xkermit ckermit
cap     Four more Kermits.  The collection keeps every build it found, and
cap     they do not all take the same command line -- which is the reason
cap     to keep them side by side.
cap     All four are asked for; two were until 2026-08-30.
run     kermit2
wait    8
run     xkermit
wait    8
run     kermit3
wait    8
run     ckermit
wait    10

shot    mail
for     mail lmail rmail smail
cap     The mail chain.  `mail' reaches for a scratch file at /r0 -- the RAM
cap     disk os9exec cannot provide -- and stops; `smail', the router,
cap     answers properly and shows what it takes.
cap     `lmail' delivers locally and `rmail' takes what uucp brought; both
cap     were credited here and never typed until 2026-08-30.
run     mail -?
wait    6
run     smail -?
wait    6
run     lmail -?
wait    6
run     rmail -?
wait    6

shot    mailx
for     mailx philmail
cap     mailx looks your mailbox up by USER NUMBER, not by $USER: this
cap     session is 0.0, which SYS/password calls `su', and the mailbox that
cap     ships belongs to `tester'.  So it says it cannot change to your
cap     mailbox directory, and the listing underneath shows the one that is
cap     there.  Log in as tester and it finds it.
run     mailx
wait    8
run     ls -l /dd/SPOOL/MAIL
wait    6
run     philmail < /nil
wait    8

shot    network
size    36 80
for     msntp atp infoxpress
cap     The other three of the network set: `msntp' sets the clock from a
cap     time server, `atp' is AppleTalk, and `infoxpress' opens a serial
cap     device and waits -- it was filed as mute until 2026-08-29 and is
cap     not.  Same missing /dd/startup.net as the `finger' card.
run     msntp -?
wait    8
run     atp
wait    8
run     infoxpress
wait    10

shot    news-tools
size    36 80
for     rnews postnews subscribe unsubscribe expire uulog
cap     What arrives from a news feed and what handles it, each asked for
cap     itself: `rnews' takes a batch, `postnews' sends one, `subscribe'
cap     and `unsubscribe' edit your groups, `expire' deletes what is past
cap     its date, `uulog' shows the transfer log.  Four of the six were
cap     credited to this card and never typed on it until 2026-08-30.
run     rnews -?
wait    8
run     postnews -?
wait    8
run     subscribe -?
wait    8
run     unsubscribe -?
wait    8
run     expire -?
wait    8
run     uulog -?
wait    8

shot    newshist
size    36 80
for     newshist bdecode byteflip c7decode dbz newslock
cap     CMDS/NEWS: the pieces that unpack a usenet news batch, each asked
cap     for itself -- `bdecode' and `c7decode' are the decoders,
cap     `byteflip' swaps endianness, `dbz' and `newshist' are the history
cap     database, `newslock' the lock the rest take.  Four of the six were
cap     credited here and never run until 2026-08-30.
run     dbz
wait    8
run     bdecode
wait    8
run     c7decode
wait    8
run     byteflip
wait    8
run     newshist
wait    8
run     newslock
wait    8

shot    pathalias
size    36 80
for     pathalias makedb dotilde
cap     `pathalias' computes mail routes from a map of who calls whom --
cap     the program that made a usenet address possible.  `makedb' builds
cap     the database it writes into, and `dotilde' expands the ~-notation
cap     a uucp path uses.  All three are asked for here; two of them were
cap     only credited until 2026-08-29, on this card and on mailx's.
run     pathalias -?
wait    8
run     makedb -?
wait    6
run     dotilde -?
wait    6

shot    screen
for     screen tsmon2 connect uwho wysetime
cap     `screen' here is NOT the terminal multiplexer -- it is Russ Smith's
cap     `screens', which picks a file at random from $HOME/.SCREENS and
cap     shows it, and on OS-9 RUNS the file through system().  There is no
cap     .SCREENS directory on this disk, so it says so.  tsmon2 beside it
cap     is what watches a serial line for someone logging in.
cap     `connect' dials out, `uwho' lists who is logged in and `wysetime'
cap     sets a Wyse terminal's clock; all three were credited here and
cap     never run until 2026-08-30.
run     screen -v
wait    8
run     tsmon2 -?
wait    8
run     connect -?
wait    8
run     uwho
wait    8
run     wysetime
wait    8

shot    sterm
size    36 80
for     sterm tterm tsu
cap     The terminal programs: `sterm' and `tterm' open a serial line and
cap     give you the far end, `tsu' switches user on one.  With no line
cap     configured they say so.  Six more used to be credited here and
cap     never typed; they are on the card below.
run     sterm
wait    8
run     tterm
wait    8
run     tsu
wait    8

shot    sterm-tools
size    36 80
for     k z blastem dld uld vmod_trap
cap     The six that travelled with sterm on its card without ever being
cap     run there.  `k' and `z' are kermit and zmodem shorthands, `dld' and
cap     `uld' download and upload, `blastem' drives a Blast link, and
cap     `vmod_trap' is the modem trap handler the rest of them call.
run     k -?
wait    6
run     z -?
wait    6
run     dld -?
wait    6
run     uld -?
wait    6
run     blastem -?
wait    6
run     vmod_trap
wait    6

shot    uuclean
size    36 80
for     uuclean uupoll disable enable
cap     The UUCP housekeeping: clean the spool, poll a site, take a device
cap     out of service and put it back.  `enable' has to be asked for by
cap     PATH -- it is a bash builtin too, and the builtin answers first.
run     uuclean -?
wait    8
run     uupoll -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/enable -?
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/disable -?
wait    6

shot    uucp-helpers
size    36 80
for     fileserv fixtext lcasep nptx pwparse
cap     FIVE PROGRAMS THAT SAY NOTHING.  These are the UUCP helpers --
cap     `fileserv' answers file requests, `fixtext' repairs a mangled text
cap     transfer, `lcasep' lower-cases a name for mail, `nptx' expands a
cap     full name into address candidates, `pwparse' reads the password
cap     file for the rest.  Every one of them prints NOTHING: bare, with an
cap     argument, with -? and with -x, measured 2026-08-29.  They are real
cap     modules and they exit cleanly -- the listing below shows all five --
cap     they simply have no UUCP spool to act on and do not say so.  Were
cap     credited to the uuclean card and never typed there, so this blank
cap     is the first honest look anyone has had at them.
run     ls -l /dd/CMDS/UUCP/fileserv /dd/CMDS/UUCP/nptx
wait    12
run     fileserv
wait    6
run     fixtext
wait    6
run     lcasep Smith
wait    6
run     nptx "John Q Public"
wait    6
run     pwparse
wait    8

shot    uucp
for     uucp uux uuxqt uucico
cap     The transfer set: uucp queues a copy, uux queues a command, uuxqt
cap     runs what a remote site queued here, uucico does the talking.
cap     All four are asked for; two were until 2026-08-30.  `uuxqt' cannot
cap     start -- it looks for a module called `procs' to see whether it is
cap     already running, and this disk has none.
run     uucp -?
wait    6
run     uucico -?
wait    6
run     uux -?
wait    6
run     uuxqt
wait    8

shot    uuname
cap     uuname lists the UUCP sites this system could reach, out of the
cap     configuration in SYS.
run     uuname
wait    4
run     uulog
wait    4

shot    uwho
for     uwho udate input
cap     What is left of the UNAXCESS bulletin board: uwho wants /etc/utmp,
cap     which OS-9 has no equivalent of.
run     uwho
wait    6
run     udate
wait    6
run     input < /nil
wait    8

shot    wn
for     wn wndex authwn inetd inetdc
cap     WN, a web server, and its indexer -- on a 68000 OS-9 disk.  It is
cap     configured to be started by inetd, which is also here.
cap     `authwn' is its authenticator and `inetd'/`inetdc' the superserver
cap     and its control program; the three were credited here without ever
cap     being typed until 2026-08-30.
run     wn -V
wait    8
run     wndex -?
wait    8
run     authwn -?
wait    8
run     inetd -?
wait    8
run     inetdc -?
wait    8

shot    xmodem
size    36 80
for     xy xydown xyt txmod rxmod
cap     The xmodem and ymodem set from CMDS/COMMS -- send, receive, and the
cap     terminal side of a file transfer over a serial line.  All five are
cap     asked for here; two of them were only credited until 2026-08-29.
cap     `xy' is asked for LAST and with -?: bare, it starts receiving and
cap     waits for a sender that will never come, which takes the session
cap     down and leaves nothing to photograph.
run     xydown
wait    8
run     xyt
wait    8
run     txmod
wait    8
run     rxmod
wait    8
run     xy -?
wait    8


shot    terminals
for     cls fkeys initvdu setterm wysecrack wysetime
cap     The small terminal utilities, asked what they take.  `fkeys' and
cap     `initvdu' print NOTHING at all -- no usage, no error -- which is
cap     what the two blank lines under their names are.  The Wyse pair want
cap     real Wyse hardware.
run     setterm -?
wait    8
run     fkeys -?
wait    8
run     wysecrack
wait    8
run     wysetime -?
wait    8
run     cls
wait    6
run     initvdu -?
wait    8
